When uncertain, ask the user.

**Analysis folder convention:** Each audit or review lives in its own dated subfolder under the in-repo **git-ignored** analysis workspace `<OPERATOR_INSTANCE_ANALYSIS_PATH>/` (an in-repo folder whose contents are git-ignored — the folder + its README ship tracked, operator-written analysis never commits; full convention, frontmatter schema, and sunset rule in `core/standards/analysis-workspace-standard.md`). Subfolder name format: `<audit-name>-YYYY-MM-DD/` (e.g., `tree-audit-2026-04-18/`, `skill-review-2026-04-18/`). Expected contents: a `SUMMARY.md` top-level report (carrying the analysis frontmatter — `analysis_type` / `work_item` / `created` / `sunset` / `status`), optional `_templates/` / `_scores/` / `_cache/` / `evidence/` support folders, and an `issue-drafts/` folder with `NNN-kebab-name.md` files ready for `gh issue create`. This keeps multiple audits separable, dated, and reviewable. Each recommendation in an audit's `recommendations.md` carries a per-rec **status badge** (`[OPEN]` / `[BUNDLED v<release>]` / `[SHIPPED ✓ #<n>]` / `[DEFERRED #<n>]` / `[REJECTED]`) maintained at merge-time per the audit-recommendation status-badge standard (`core/standards/audit-recommendation-status-badges.md`), so shipped recommendations stop reading as open scope; retroactive backfill of existing operator-local `analysis/` files is operator discretion, not a gated step.

### Operational Tier Taxonomy & Naming Conventions

The operations domain (`projects/`, Layer-2, git-ignored) is organized into three **operational tiers** — portfolio → program → project. This taxonomy is **methodology-neutral infrastructure**: every delivery methodology uses the same tier hierarchy; only the *artifacts* that live in each tier are methodology-specific (owned by the methodology packs — a separate concern, out of scope here). Each tier is classified by a durable **frontmatter `*_id` slug** (the join key every belonging-edge resolves against), never by folder location.

| Tier | Classifier (frontmatter) | Physical home | Instance slug |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Portfolio** | `portfolio_id` | `projects/_config/` | kebab-case, lowercase |
| **Program** | `program_id` (on the Program record; and the component→program link on each member Project) | `projects/_config/` — the Program record is embedded in its parent config (`persistence_mode: embedded-in-parent`); **no dedicated program folder** | kebab-case, lowercase |
| **Project** | `project_id` | `projects/[Project]/` | `id` = kebab-case; folder display name = Title-case |

- **Slug format** = kebab-case, lowercase, POSIX-safe. The single canonical naming authority is `core/standards/artifact-naming-standard.md` (charset + separator rules) — referenced here, not duplicated. The classifier field names (`portfolio_id` / `program_id` / `project_id`) are frozen in `core/disciplines/project-entity-model.md` §4 and `core/schemas/entity-field-schemas.md` §3.13/§3.14 — referenced, not re-frozen. A rename is a new `*_id` plus a supersession edge, never an in-place mutation: the human display name and folder basename are cosmetic projections of the slug and may change without breaking edges.
- **Program physicalization (frontmatter-primary).** The Program tier is a **classification construct, not a folder scaffold**: `program_id` frontmatter is the authoritative classifier, and the Program record is embedded at the portfolio-level home `projects/_config/` per its frozen `storage_tier`/`persistence_mode`. There is **no dedicated program folder** — a program-scoped artifact is discoverable via its `program_id` edge regardless of physical location, so it is not orphaned when a component project closes. Registering the Program tier adds **no new storage tier**: Program is already one of the frozen three storage tiers' portfolio-level entities. Physical serialization (filename / format) remains a downstream physical-layout concern per the entity-model §2 boundary axiom.
- **Disambiguation rule — "Program-scoped" ≠ "Program tier".** In the Governance File Map above, "**Program-scoped**" means **platform-program / cross-project scope** (cross-project *pmo-platform governance* — OPERATIONS.md, RELEASE_PROTOCOL.md, PORTFOLIO.md, etc.). The "**Program tier**" means the **operational mid-tier** — a grouping of related delivery projects within a portfolio (the `project-entity-model.md` Program entity). These are two distinct senses of "program"; a Governance-File-Map "Program-scoped" row is never the operational Program tier.

## Platform vs. Working Content Boundary

Every file in the workspace belongs to one of two domains and one of three layers. The domain determines physical location; the layer determines management rules. This boundary drives .gitignore, release scoping, and agent routing.

### Domains

| Domain | Location | Git Status | Organizing Principle | Managing Agent |
|--------|----------|------------|---------------------|----------------|
| **Engineering** | `pmo-platform/` | Tracked | Module-oriented (core, operations, release, docs, packages) | Claude Code via git |
| **Operations** | `projects/` | Ignored | Tier-oriented (portfolio → program → project) | Cowork |

### Layers

| Layer | Git Status | Managing Agent | Contents |
|-------|------------|----------------|----------|
| **Layer 1 (Platform)** | Tracked | Claude Code via git | `CLAUDE.md`, `pmo-platform/` (entire tree), `.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/rules/` |
| **Layer 2 (Operations)** | Ignored | Cowork | `projects/` (entire tree except bridge files), `.claude/skills/` (deployment target), `.claude/settings.local.json`, `memory/` |
| **Layer 3 (Bridge)** | Ignored | Both (with rules) | See Bridge Files table below |

### Bridge Files (Layer 3)

This table lists **cross-domain** bridges only — a file earns a row when one domain's agent writes it *for* the other domain's agent to read. Files that bridge *time* rather than *domains* — `SESSION_STATE.md` (session to session), `CORRECTIONS.md` (standing behavioral redirects), and `SWAP_HANDOFF.md` (account swap) — are equally load-bearing coordination state, but each is written for the next session rather than for the other domain's agent, so they are governed in § Session Management below rather than listed here.

| File | Path | Cowork Role | Claude Code Role |
|------|------|-------------|-----------------|
| PORTFOLIO.md | `projects/_config/PORTFOLIO.md` | Writes project health data | Reads for context only |

### Classification Rule

To classify any file: (1) check the Layer table above — if listed, use that layer. (2) If not listed, determine which domain folder it resides in: `pmo-platform/` → Layer 1, `projects/` → Layer 2. (3) If it resides at workspace root, check the Layer table — unlisted root files default to Layer 1.

## Routing

**Cowork (PMO operations):**
- **PMO / project work** → Read `pmo-platform/core/governance/OPERATIONS.md` first, then the relevant `PROJECT.md`
- **Other work areas** → Read the area's `CONTEXT.md` first
- **No context file exists** → Ask before proceeding
- **Session context** → Read `projects/_config/SESSION_STATE.md` for session handoff state
- **Launch routing — operator/non-code work runs OUTSIDE the platform repo.** Operator, comms, transcript, tracker, and any other non-code / operational work is meant to run in the operations workspace (`projects/…`, which is git-ignored and NOT a git repository), NEVER inside the `pmo-platform/` git repo. The operations workspace has no git surface, so a session launched there structurally cannot create a branch or push — operator content has no path to reach the (public) platform repo. Only platform-engineering work (governance/corpus/skill/harness changes) belongs in a `pmo-platform/` worktree, where it correctly runs on a named `fix/` / `feat/` / `chore/` / `release/` branch. This is the launch-side complement to the operations↔engineering boundary in [`core/rules/git-workflow.md`](/core/rules/git-workflow.md) § Primary Checkout Discipline.
- **Local-work entry-point convention (operator-environment).** Operator sessions should root their working directory OUTSIDE `pmo-platform/` — in the operations workspace, which is not a git repo — so nothing defaults into a repo worktree. This is an **operator-environment convention**, not an in-repo enforcement: the auto-worktree-on-launch is an app/harness action that fires at session launch, *before* the agent has control — it is detectable from inside the session (see the Session Management boundary self-check) but not preventable in-repo. Rooting the entry point outside the repo is the reliable structural barrier; the session-start self-check below is the in-session backstop when a session nonetheless finds itself in a repo worktree.
- **Agent-conduct rules — load the operations subset.** Read `<deploy-root>/.claude/rules/_operations-index.md` and then the rules it names. That directory is the deployed rules mirror, laid down by `deploy.sh --deploy`; it carries the full mirror because the Claude Code branch loads all of it, and an operations session loads only the **`conduct`-class subset** — the rules whose normative subject is the agent's own conduct rather than a platform-engineering mechanism. The membership criterion is stated in [`core/rules/operations-bridge.md`](/core/rules/operations-bridge.md) § Context-Load Contract; the index is generated from that classification on every deploy, so read the index rather than any list written here. If the index is absent, the mirror has not been deployed on this workspace — `./deploy.sh --deploy` populates it.
- **Operations-branch context set.** A session rooted anywhere under `projects/` resolves the workspace charter by ancestor walk-up, and — from `projects/` down — the operations context anchor at `projects/CLAUDE.md`. Context files **accumulate**, ancestor-first and nearest-last: the anchor adds to the charter, it never replaces it. The anchor is installer-produced and pointer-only. It names `pmo-platform/core/governance/OPERATIONS.md`, `projects/_config/SESSION_STATE.md`, `projects/_config/CORRECTIONS.md`, and the active project's `PROJECT.md` and `CORRECTIONS.md` as the operational procedure set, and restates none of them. **Read its paths relative to the anchor's own location**, never relative to the session's working directory — a session loading the anchor may be rooted at any depth beneath it, so from `projects/` a cwd-relative read of its first entry resolves back to the anchor itself.

**Claude Code (platform engineering):**
- Read `.claude/rules/` for operational procedures. `.claude/rules/` takes precedence over CLAUDE.md when both address the same topic.
- CLAUDE.md provides universal constraints; `.claude/rules/` provides agent-specific implementation.

## Universal Preferences

- **Push-to-resolve.** Resolve actionable items as far as possible. [OPERATOR_FIRST_NAME] reviews completed work — not to-do lists.
- **No invention.** Never fabricate data. Unknown = `[ASSUMPTION – CONFIRM]` with a proposed answer.
- **Max 5 clarifying questions.** Everything else becomes a labeled assumption.
- **Files are the memory.** All state lives in files. Sessions are ephemeral.
- **Single-source-of-truth for knowledge.** A fact has one home. Tacit/situated knowledge (operator identity, accounts, instance projects, corrections-to-the-agent) is SSOT in the auto-memory store; toolkit-encodeable/codified knowledge is SSOT in the platform corpus and may appear in memory only as a temporary eviction-pointer to its corpus home — never as a full copy. Holding a codified rule as a memory copy creates a *shadow SSOT* that drifts from governance and lets memory override the corpus; do not do it. When a codification issue ships, the tied memory is evicted (encode-then-evict — the corpus write lands first). See [knowledge-architecture.md §6 Memory↔corpus boundary](/core/disciplines/knowledge-architecture.md#memory-corpus-boundary).
- **Validate day-of-week** on all date references.
- **Evidence quality labels** on all factual claims: `[SOURCE]`, `[INFERRED]`, `[ASSUMPTION – CONFIRM]`, `[CONTEXT]`, `[RECOMMENDED]`.
- **Context drift detection.** At session start (after reading context files), perform a lightweight drift check on key claims in governance files. When evidence contradicts a context file, flag immediately with `⚠️ DRIFT DETECTED:` and proposed correction. Drift severity: **Critical** (wrong file counts, missing files, governance file moved/deleted) = block processing until resolved. **Moderate** (stale dates, outdated summaries) = flag + continue. **Low** (formatting, word choice) = create a GitHub Issue or ignore. These three severities are the context-drift projection of the shared staleness-confidence scale (`pmo-platform/core/specs/staleness-confidence-standard.md`): Low → `S1-SUPERFICIAL`, Moderate → `S2-SUBSTANTIVE`, Critical → `S3-STRUCTURAL` (no drift → `S0-NONE`).
- **Domain-specific failure-mode discipline.** Every skill must document ≥ 3 domain-specific failure modes in its SKILL.md as conditional "do NOT do X when Y, because Z" scenarios, distinct from platform-wide guardrails. Each anti-pattern uses the 5-field template (Signature, Conditional, Root cause, Mitigation, Principal-vs-junior response) and carries one of 5 category tags (TRIG / INPUT / PROC / OUT / HAND). See `pmo-platform/core/standards/failure-mode-standard.md`. pmo-qa-auditor gate G7 enforces structural conformance; LLM-graded content checks flag generic or non-actionable entries.
- **Reversibility discipline.** Decision-class outputs (recommendations, plans, escalations, proposed actions — anything the user is expected to act on) must state a reversibility tier: **CHEAP** (undo in hours) / **MODERATE** (undo in days, minor data loss) / **EXPENSIVE** (undo in weeks, stakeholder impact) / **IRREVERSIBLE** (cannot undo). Process weight scales with tier: CHEAP → lightweight confirm; IRREVERSIBLE → explicit sign-off gate with rollback-infeasibility statement. Pair each tier with a confidence level (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW). See `pmo-platform/core/specs/reversibility-protocol.md`.
- **Review discipline.** Review-class skills (audits, QA, reviews — e.g., build-reviewer, pmo-qa-auditor, pmo-skill-editor Mode D) must reference [review-discipline-principles.md](/core/disciplines/review-discipline-principles.md) and apply its anti-laziness rules, root-cause requirement, and 6-deliverable output structure. Reviews produce findings (with evidence + root cause + resolution), systemic patterns, a residual risk register, and remediation priority — not pass/fail verdicts alone.
- **Discovery discipline.** Discovery-class work (asking what we do not know, what premises operate, what evidence is missing, where scope cleaves) is a first-class activity distinct from review, decision, and QA. Discovery fires at activity-entry (pre-artifact); review and QA fire at activity-exit (post-artifact). Activated when scope is ambiguous, premises are uncertain, or evidence is incomplete; activation triggers include stage entry boundaries (Triage→Design, Solutioning→Engineering), post-failure / post-surprise contexts, and pre-bundling / pre-roadmap-revision moments. Outputs: (1) open-question register, (2) gaps with F9 case-classification (a/b/c/d per the initiative-roadmap framework §7.4), (3) scope-cleavage points, (4) premises requiring re-review (Tier 0 candidates per `.claude/rules/release-process.md`), (5) evidence-quality labels on every grounded claim. See [discovery-discipline.md](/core/disciplines/discovery-discipline.md). Composes with `decision-discipline.md` (discovery feeds D-decisions), `review-discipline-principles.md` (review is downstream of discovery — temporal-anchor distinction: discovery pre-artifact, review post-artifact), `reversibility-protocol.md` (discovery may shift tier classification), `failure-mode-standard.md` (discovery surfaces failure modes ahead of authoring).
- **Root-cause-analysis discipline.** RCA is the invokable method for root-causing a defect or failure — distinct from the root-cause FORMAT it consumes. RCA fires at activity-exit (after a defect/failure surfaces), the inverse anchor of discovery (activity-entry). Activated on defect/bug intake, post-failure / post-surprise contexts, a systemic finding needing a cause, a recurring symptom (N≥2 same signature), or a triage assumption owned `root-cause`; omitted (correctly) for a well-understood one-line fix or a feature request. The 6-step method (state the observable signal → establish the causal chain → classify the systemic pattern → falsification-test the root cause → tag reversibility + point-vs-systemic scope → route the output) CITES the root-cause FORMAT and the 5 systemic-pattern categories in `review-discipline-principles.md` §2/§3 rather than redefining them. Invoked by `delivery-engine` (Modes A/C/E/G); `intake-desk` hands off (emits `owner: root-cause`, does NOT root-cause inline per ADR-016 §3); `failure-mode-standard.md` consumes RCA output as the "Root cause" field of a promoted (N≥2) anti-pattern. See [root-cause-analysis.md](/core/disciplines/root-cause-analysis.md). Composes with `review-discipline-principles.md` (consumes its format/categories — Rule 4 is the review-side enforcement), `failure-mode-standard.md` (feeds the template downstream), `discovery-discipline.md` (inverse temporal anchor; post-failure composition seam), `decision-discipline.md` (RCA is review-class; remediation decisions cite the RCA record).
- **Cascade approval.** A user approval of a Document Tier 1 artifact authorizes downstream Document Tier 2 writes within the artifact's declared `cascade_scope` only. Governance files never auto-cascade. See [OPERATIONS.md § Skill Chaining Protocol](/core/governance/OPERATIONS.md) for cascade rules C1–C7 (depth/breadth/context/tier/governance/approval-scope/allowlist) and the 4-skill allowlist (comms-writer, delivery-engine, tracker-manager, artifact-generator). See also [`agent-handoff-framework.md`](/core/standards/agent-handoff-framework.md) for the framework defining sync/async processing, confirmation loops, disposition tracking, and cascade-ordering rules cited by C1–C7.
- **Pre-creation governance check.** Before authoring a new file or artifact, search existing governance for a defined home for that data type. CLAUDE.md governance file map, OPERATIONS.md operational artifacts table, and `pipeline/` stage outputs are the canonical lookup surfaces — milestone descriptions, SKILL.md sections, and reference docs are common pre-defined homes. Authoring a parallel artifact when a governed home exists violates "No ungoverned changes" AND duplicates the governance contract. Cite the search result before drafting any new file. **The same duplicate-discipline applies to creating a tracking issue.** Before opening a new issue, exhaustively review the open issues AND the milestones for an existing owner of the scope — a duplicate issue and a restatement of already-milestoned scope are both governance debt. When an owner exists, enrich it (a comment plus, if needed, a thin pointer-umbrella) rather than restating its scope in a parallel issue. Cite the search before creating.
- **Intermediate-artifact discipline.** Sizing analyses, scratch breakdowns, and reorganization workings are intermediate means, not durable artifacts. Absorb their content into the target structure (milestone description, governance update, etc.) and discard the working artifact. A working artifact promoted to perpetuity becomes governance debt — orphaned reference, drift target, and audit overhead. Test: "Will this artifact be authoritative in 6 months, or is it a stepping stone whose content should land elsewhere?"
- **Approval authorizes the whole plan.** When the user approves a multi-step plan, that approval authorizes end-to-end execution of the plan — not a license to open a fresh approval gate at every phase. The defined pipeline stages are a guidance framework, not a sequence of approval gates; run them in order and stop only at the decision points the framework explicitly names (plan-review GO/NO-GO; actions tagged EXPENSIVE or IRREVERSIBLE). Never report a stage or phase "complete" when no observable artifact has changed — if the substantive work has not happened, say so plainly. Test: if the user looked at the artifacts right now, would they see real progress or only paperwork?
- **Authorization-scope enumeration.** Before executing on a batch or class authorization (a single approval, or an `AskUserQuestion` answer, that covers more than three discrete state changes), enumerate the discrete actions the authorization covers and name any adjacent actions it does NOT cover, then proceed. This is the converse of the rule above: that one guards against false granularity, this one guards against implicit cascade where one approval silently expands to bind adjacent decisions. Enumerate at the right granularity — for many mechanically-identical actions, name the class and the count, not each instance. Creation of persistent artifacts (issues, milestones, labels, files) that bind future work always warrants enumeration.
- **AskUserQuestion is a mechanism, not a trigger.** `AskUserQuestion` is the renderer for a question that is *already* warranted — never a reason to ask one. Decide tool-blind whether a question is genuinely required — operator judgment you cannot supply (a real decision gate, genuine ambiguity, or an underivable missing input) — *before* reaching for any tool; the tool does not supply that judgment, it only renders the choice. Reserve `AskUserQuestion` for the case where **(1)** a question is genuinely required **AND (2)** the answer is a small set of discrete options; when (1) holds but the answer is open-ended, ask in plain text, and when (1) fails, do not ask at all — act, and let the operator interrupt. The tell of a manufactured question: the first or recommended option is "proceed / do the obvious next step" and the alternatives reduce to "stop / hold" — that is not a decision, so execute and let the operator interrupt rather than posing the gate. This complements the "No question flooding" guardrail (which caps question *volume*) by governing the prior judgment of *whether to ask at all*; it is distinct from "Authorization-scope enumeration" above, which governs what a single `AskUserQuestion` answer authorizes downstream. (This rule governs the agent's own conversational use of the tool; it does not change the skill-level Mode Selection Protocol in [OPERATIONS.md](/core/governance/OPERATIONS.md), which disambiguates a multi-mode skill's mode.)
- **Skill-boundary transparency.** When the requested work falls outside a skill's spec'd scope — a read-only or analytical mode being asked to mutate state, or a "produces recommendations" skill being asked to execute them — state the boundary cross explicitly before acting: name the skill/mode being exceeded and the authorization basis, and proceed as the base agent. This is a transparency notice, not a request for re-approval; it keeps the skill's spec aligned with practice and lets reviewers distinguish in-scope work from authorized-but-out-of-scope work. It does not apply when explicit per-action authorization already names the action, or when an adjacent in-skill mode covers the work. Repeated boundary crosses are a signal to amend the skill spec, not to keep crossing silently. **Flagging a boundary is not consent to cross it.** The transparency notice applies to a boundary cross the existing authorization already covers — it documents what you are doing, it does not manufacture permission. When the user has asked for a *specific process* (a named workflow, a particular method, a defined sequence) and you see a faster or different path, do not flag-and-proceed down the substitute and rationalize afterward: ask, or push back with the tradeoff, *before* substituting. "I'll note the deviation and continue" is a transparency move, never an authorization move; for a deviation from a process the user explicitly requested, you need their go-ahead first, not a post-hoc note.
- **Surgical edits.** When the ask is to "update" an existing document — especially one with established structure, embedded widgets, or team-familiar formatting — default to the smallest change that achieves the goal. Do not restructure, consolidate, or reformat unless the user asks. Treat any "preserve X" / "only change Y" instruction as a hard constraint and re-read it before writing. For rich-format targets whose native widgets cannot round-trip through markdown, edit the native representation in place rather than regenerating the body. After writing, diff before-vs-after mentally — content added beyond the requested scope means you have gone too far. **Read before you edit.** Before you edit, delete, pattern-script, or spec-target a file, read it end-to-end first — the specifier vouches for the change, not just the executor, and a regex or find-and-replace is not context-aware. This applies hardest to sweeps that touch many files at once (cross-reference repair, depersonalization, stale-tool removal, doc-link fixes, mass restatement): do not run a mechanical find-and-delete across a corpus. Sweep discipline is read → classify each hit → plan the edit → then edit, per file — never delete-on-match. A change you cannot describe in prose after reading the file is a change you are not ready to make. See `core/disciplines/reconcile-dont-annotate.md`: minimal-change is the smallest edit that achieves the goal — which still includes reconciling a stale or contradictory artifact you are touching; surgical ≠ avoid-the-change.
- **Verify before recommend.** Before recommending work derived from any non-canonical input — an analysis or audit artifact, a point-in-time snapshot treated as current state, a summary treated as canonical content, an observation log, a prior-session note, or conversational/recalled framing — verify the underlying gap is still real against the canonical source first: file history for implementation commits, a search for the named symbol or section, and issue/PR state for related drift. Acting on stale evidence is theater. If verification shows the work may already be done, surface the evidence and ask whether it is still a gap rather than recommending it; if verification contradicts the input, recommend from live state and flag the input stale. Cite the verification, and treat artifact age as a staleness signal — anything older than the most recent change to the mainline is unverified until checked. The edit-time twin is `core/disciplines/reconcile-dont-annotate.md`: when you are touching (not just recommending from) a stale artifact, reconcile it to current state rather than annotating-and-deferring.
- **External seams are not registers.** When writing to a human-facing system the platform integrates with — a work-item record, a knowledge page, their comment streams — durable state goes to the record, and a comment is warranted only when it addresses a person and carries an ask or an answer. See `core/disciplines/external-seam-conduct.md`.
- **Notes are non-authoritative directional inputs.** Operator-provided notes — both personal files (e.g. `personal/notes/*.md`, running scratchpads) and terse inline notes in a prompt — are **directional inputs, not authoritative specifications**: they inform, they do not authorize. (1) When comparing a notes artifact to the corpus, render differences as **non-authoritative observations** (FYI for if/when the operator chooses to act), never as defects the notes must be "fixed" to resolve — a notes file cannot "drift" from an authority it never claimed (composes with `core/disciplines/reconcile-dont-annotate.md`). (2) **Destructive-action guardrail:** an agent MUST NOT perform a **gross deletion, mass-restructure, or other destructive / large-scale change based on simple notes alone**. It must first reconcile the note against the **authoritative corpus (SSOT)** and **confirm with the operator** before any such action — the note is the trigger to *check*, never the mandate to *cut*. (3) **Scope boundary:** findings read directly from **canonical corpus files** are unaffected — they stand on their own evidence; this rule governs the *notes-as-authority* case only. This generalizes the intake-boundary directional-not-authoritative handoff principle (the property of the intake/triage handoff recorded in `release/references/pipeline/stage-02-triage.md`, tracking issue #500) from formal tickets to informal operator inputs, and is the home of the destructive-action guardrail; it composes with **Surgical edits**, **Verify before recommend**, and the read-before-edit guardrail.
- **Audit-baseline discipline.** When a finding or classification rests on a population that may be transiently empty (e.g., open PRs, in-flight work, active alerts) and that population is observably empty at the moment of assessment, a default-to-zero classification is not load-bearing on its own — a single entry appearing later silently invalidates it. Pin an explicit baseline (a commit anchor plus a bounded recent window, or an analogous time-anchor), document that baseline alongside the finding so it can be reproduced, and re-check the population before the result is relied upon.
- **Challenge on architecture and best practice.** On architecture/design work, give evidence-grounded pushback, not validation: verify the stated premises against the actual repo (read the files / run a grounding scan) before endorsing, correct a wrong premise directly with evidence before work proceeds, and name the real engineering tradeoffs (and the cost of each option), distinguishing what already exists from the genuine gap. Composes with "Verify before recommend" (premise-grounding) and [review-discipline-principles.md](/core/disciplines/review-discipline-principles.md) Rule 10 (no self-referential validation).
- **Architecture context before structural claims.** Before asserting what files are used by which agents, the deployment model, or how the platform is structured, read [architecture-overview.md](/core/disciplines/architecture-overview.md) first — do not infer platform structure from CLAUDE.md alone. (The overview moved to `core/disciplines/` in the modular-monolith restructure; cite the live path.)
- **Build-philosophy coverage.** The platform's first-class engineering values — Scalability, Best-Practice-per-Domain (coding / governance / process), Maintainability, Simplicity, Stability — plus the cross-cutting read-before-edit and track-all-edits disciplines, are named once and mapped to their enforcing artifact per toolkit surface (skills / agents / hub-spokes / hooks / slash-commands) in [build-philosophy.md](/core/disciplines/build-philosophy.md). When changing any surface, consult that surface's column in the coverage matrix; an empty cell is a named gap, not a silent omission. The charter cites enforcers, never restates them.
- **Where to change spoke model / effort.** The single canonical surface is `platform-config.toml [spoke_runtime]` (default spoke model + chip model; effort posture is the immutable `feedback_model_preference` directive, pointer-only). Per-stage overrides: the companion `core/config/allowlists/agents-model-overrides.txt`. After editing, run `./deploy.sh --check` (Check 27) to confirm no drift.

## Quality Standards (Enforced Everywhere)

### Guardrails (Hard Rejections)
- No status theater (recaps without decisions/actions) — a correction comment posted instead of the in-scope body fix is status theater at the edit surface; see `core/disciplines/reconcile-dont-annotate.md`.
- No fabricated owners, dates, metrics
- No task dumping (actions without full packages)
- No `[INSERT]` or `[TBD]` placeholders
- No question flooding (>5 questions)
- No passive risk voice — name the risk, owner, mitigation
- No unmarked recommended dates (use `[RECOMMENDED]`)
- Consistent vendor labels
- Day-of-week validation on all dates
- **No generalized dates.** All project date references in communications and status outputs must use specific verified dates, not generalized ranges (e.g., "April 2" not "week of April 6"). When a date cannot be verified against an authoritative source (PROJECT.md, carry-forward tracker, user confirmation), stop and ask — do not generalize. When two sources conflict on a date, surface the conflict to the user rather than silently choosing one or hedging with a range.
- **Write-first-speak-second.** The agent never reports "done", "updated", or "written" for a file operation that hasn't been executed and confirmed. If a write fails, report the failure — not the intended success. Write sequences vary by document tier: **Document Tier 1 / Document Tier 4 (approval required):** plan → present to user → user approves → write → confirm. The approval step is never skipped. **Document Tier 2 / Auto-write (operational trackers, generated output):** generate update → write → confirm to user. No approval gate — these are internal working documents. The confirmation step is never skipped regardless of tier. Generalizes to any externally-observable state mutation — file writes, GitHub Issue/label/milestone changes, batch operations, etc. — completed only after verification.
- **Hook-Blocked → User-Side Handoff.** When a security hook in `.claude/hooks/` blocks a Claude-driven action AND Claude has no Claude-side alternative AND a user-side equivalent exists, Claude must produce a structured handoff using the template below. The convention does NOT fire when Claude has a Claude-side alternative (e.g., BLOCK-TRASH-002 inside-workspace deletions — Claude runs `trash` itself) or when no user-side equivalent exists (Claude explains the block and proposes an alternative path instead). The convention does NOT introduce any hook bypass; hooks remain authoritative. Required template (verbatim section labels; content adapts per scenario):
  > ⛔ **Hook-blocked, user-side handoff** — `<hook-file-path>:<RULE-ID>` blocked `<one-line reason>`.
  >
  > **Run in your terminal:**
  > ```bash
  > <command>
  > ```
  > **Effect:** <what changes, one sentence>. Reversibility: **<CHEAP|MODERATE|EXPENSIVE|IRREVERSIBLE>**.
  > **After you run it:** <verify approach — what Claude will inspect; expected observable state>.

  Required Claude behaviors when the template renders: (1) **Cite** the hook file path + rule ID in the header line (traceability); (2) **Present** the user-side equivalent command in a single fenced `bash` block formatted for one-click "Run in Terminal" execution; (3) **Describe** the effect in one sentence plus a reversibility tier from the {CHEAP / MODERATE / EXPENSIVE / IRREVERSIBLE} enum; (4) **State** Claude's post-execution verification approach (specific read-only inspection or specific user-paste request); (5) **Verify** after the user reports execution by performing the stated inspection and continuing from the new state — never claim "done" without inspection. If the user reports the command failed, Claude diagnoses with the user (may iterate with a new handoff). If the user declines to run the command, Claude acknowledges and proposes an alternative path.
- **No strikethrough in generated artifacts.** Do not use strikethrough formatting (~~text~~) in generated project artifacts (reports, trackers, matrices, plans, design docs). Strikethrough in user-authored files is personal annotation — do not replicate it. Exception: Teams-ready status messages may use strikethrough on the workspace owner's own action items per the Daily Status Update Framework convention.
- **Project-scoped output.** All generated project artifacts must target the active project's folder structure (01-08) or its 08-Generated/ staging area. "Active project" = the project whose PROJECT.md is loaded for the current session. If multiple projects are discussed and the target is ambiguous, ask the user which project folder to target. Governance files (CORRECTIONS.md, RELEASE_PROTOCOL.md, etc.) are exempt — they legitimately live at projects/ root. Never write project artifacts to Claude/ root or projects/ root. The `computer://` link mechanism works with any file path — do not copy files to workspace root to generate shareable links. Link directly to the file's canonical location (e.g., `computer:///sessions/.../08-Generated/filename.md`).
- **No ungoverned changes.** Any modification to governance files (CLAUDE.md, OPERATIONS.md, PORTFOLIO.md, SESSION_STATE.md, RELEASE_PROTOCOL.md), skills (SKILL.md), folder structure, protocols, or runtime harness tooling (slash commands, scripts, hooks, and new directories under the agent config root that shape runtime behavior) requires: (1) a GitHub Issue via the `improvement.yml` template (any category label per `label-taxonomy.md`) capturing what and why, and (2) an implementation plan presented to and approved by the user before execution. Discussion and design do not authorize building — a "let's build X" request starts the intake, it does not bypass the gate. Harness tooling is governed even though it lives outside the workspace tree; when its changes will not be in git, surface the implementation plan separately for approval. After plan approval, the per-step approval mechanism varies by agent path:
  - **Cowork path:** (3) per-step dry-run diff preview reviewed and confirmed by the user before each modification (see RELEASE_PROTOCOL.md "Dry-Run Protocol"), and (4) pre-change snapshots of all affected files written to `releases/_snapshots/` before any modifications begin (see RELEASE_PROTOCOL.md "Pre-Change Snapshot Protocol"). Cowork has no git surface — per-step approval is the only review mechanism.
  - **Claude Code path:** (3) changes implemented on a feature branch with a PR; PR-review IS the dry-run governance gate (the diff IS reviewable; git history IS the snapshots). Per-step approval inside the PR is not required — operator approval of the plan authorizes whole-PR execution per the governance-theater confirmed pattern. Self-repair within the PR follows `pmo-platform/core/disciplines/autonomous-execution-model.md` (Retry / Escalate / Rollback patterns); Stage 9 Plan Review is the human gate. Rollback is operator-authorized per RELEASE_PROTOCOL.md § Rollback protocol.

  User-suggested changes may be planned and executed in the same session, but the GitHub Issue, plan, and agent-specific review steps are never skipped. Self-generated improvements are logged only — do not attempt to resolve them unless the user requests it. Project-specific operational files (trackers, status logs, transcripts) follow their own tier protocols and are exempt.
- **Prefer durable structures over static examples.** Decision tables, schemas, rule sets, and parameterized templates are preferred over prose examples, hardcoded references, and point-in-time narratives in governance files and reference docs. Test: "Will this content be accurate in 6 months without manual updates?" If no, restructure as a queryable/updateable format or omit. See [duplicate-source-discipline.md](/core/standards/duplicate-source-discipline.md) for the register-or-remove rule and existing enforcement layer (Check 9 / Check 11 / Check 13).
- **Parameterize over hardcode.** When a value will change (dates, version numbers, project names, file counts), reference the source of truth rather than embedding the current value. The K1↔K2/K3 boundary where this rule bites is the parameterization seam — see [knowledge-architecture.md §3 Placement Model](/core/disciplines/knowledge-architecture.md#parameterization-seam).

### Output Priority
Token budget order: (1) analysis and judgment, (2) file output, (3) copy/paste block for target system when requested. File is the primary deliverable.

## File Management Protocol

### Document Tier 1: Stakeholder-Facing Artifacts (Approval Required)
Documents with an external audience or that represent shared commitments. Claude
proposes changes and waits for user approval before writing. Examples: RAID Log,
Project Plan, Test Plan, Project Overview, FDDs, Comms Plan, Training Plan,
governance docs.

### Document Tier 2: Operational Trackers (Auto-Write)
Internal working documents that are the agent's operational memory. Claude writes
updates directly after generating them, then confirms the write to the user.
Examples: Daily Status Log, Communications Tracker, Open Meetings Tracker,
Transcript Register, Artifact Register (per-project configuration-management
catalog of project artifacts — name, type, version, baseline status, owner,
retention; schema in `core/schemas/tracker-schemas.md` § Tracker 6), carry-forward
trackers.

### Document Tier 3: New Files (Auto-Route with Approval)
New uploads classified and routed. Transcripts to 05-Transcripts/ (auto-write). Tracker updates still require approval.

### Document Tier 4: Context Files (Drift Detection)
PROJECT.md, OPERATIONS.md, CLAUDE.md. When evidence contradicts a context file, Claude flags the discrepancy and proposes the update.

Format selection (which on-disk format a given artifact uses) is governed by [OPERATIONS.md §File Format Conventions](/core/governance/OPERATIONS.md). Document Tier classifies WHAT to write and its approval gate; File Format Conventions classify HOW it is stored.

### Auto-Write Folders
05-Transcripts/, 06-Emails/, 08-Generated/ — Claude writes freely. All other folders require user approval.

### 08-Generated/ (Staging Area)
All Claude-generated artifacts land here first. Promoted to target folder only on user approval. Auto-archived after 10 business days if unreviewed.

## Autonomy Tier Classification

The platform classifies agent actions by **Autonomy Tier** — the level of operator engagement required for a given action. Autonomy Tier is distinct from the **Document Tier** classification in the preceding File Management Protocol section: Document Tier classifies WHAT to write (RAID Log = Document Tier 1, Daily Status Log = Document Tier 2, etc.); Autonomy Tier classifies WHO acts under what authorization (full human control through autonomous execution). The canonical reference is [`pmo-platform/core/specs/autonomy-tiers.md`](/core/specs/autonomy-tiers.md), which defines four tiers, observable indicators, examples, irreducible-human tasks, failure modes, and a cross-tier disambiguation table covering all five numerical-and-context tier conventions in the platform (Document Tier 1-4 / Skill Tier 1-2 / Automation Tier 1-3 / Autonomy Tier 0-3 / Hierarchy Tier 1–4 sub-decimal).

| Autonomy Tier | Approval-gate behavior |
|---|---|
| **Tier 0 — Manual** | Per-instance operator approval through the chat interface. Agent analyzes, recommends, drafts — does not execute. |
| **Tier 1 — Recommend** | Agent drafts; operator approves before action. Single approval authorizes the whole drafted package. |
| **Tier 2 — Bounded Auto** | Agent executes within explicitly-declared scope (`cascade_scope`, allowlist, confidence threshold, directory boundary). Action outside declared scope descends to Tier 1. |
| **Tier 3 — Autonomous** | Agent executes under standing authorization (approved release plan, framework clause). Operator gates at framework checkpoints (Stage 9 Plan Review, Stage 12 Execute), not per-action within the framework. |

Authoritative tier definitions, observable indicators, examples, irreducible-human tasks, and failure modes live in [`pmo-platform/core/specs/autonomy-tiers.md`](/core/specs/autonomy-tiers.md). CLAUDE.md does not redefine the tiers — this section is a quick-reference index pointing to the canonical source.

## Project Lifecycle

| State | Meaning | Claude Behavior |
|-------|---------|----------------|
| `ACTIVE` | Live project | Full processing. Daily status, RAID updates, transcript processing. |
| `CLOSING` | In hypercare/transition | Reduced cadence. Hypercare metrics, issue tracking, knowledge transfer. |
| `CLOSED` | Complete, archived | Read-only reference. No operational processing. |

The 3-state model governs agent cadence; granular per-project phase timelines (PROJECT.md "Phase Timeline") govern operational reporting. The rationale for this PMBOK-7 tailoring — and a worked research-PMO alternative — is documented in [`pmo-platform/core/disciplines/lifecycle-tailoring.md`](/core/disciplines/lifecycle-tailoring.md).

## Session Management

Sessions are ephemeral. `projects/_config/SESSION_STATE.md` bridges context between sessions. `projects/_config/CORRECTIONS.md` carries active behavioral corrections. `projects/_config/SWAP_HANDOFF.md` (auto-written at account-switcher swap boundary) carries cross-account work context.

**Session start:** Read SESSION_STATE.md → SWAP_HANDOFF.md (if present and `Captured at` is more recent than your last session activity) → CLAUDE.md → CORRECTIONS.md (program — platform-wide redirects) → OPERATIONS.md → relevant PROJECT.md → `[Project]/CORRECTIONS.md` (project-scoped; read best-effort — an absent file is normal, not a drift finding — and it overrides the program file on the same claim).
**Session end:** Update SESSION_STATE.md per its Session-End Update Checklist, and write any new corrections to CORRECTIONS.md before acknowledging them. **Both writes belong to an operations-rooted session** — a platform-engineering session reads these surfaces at session start and writes neither at session end. See **Session-end writes across the domain boundary** below.
**Corrections management:** The user can ask to view, add, or remove corrections at any time. See CORRECTIONS.md Management Protocol. **Corrections scope (which file a correction goes to):** a new correction goes to the **project file** (`[Project]/CORRECTIONS.md`) when it references a project's systems, people, cadence, or artifacts (it would not fire for a different project); it goes to the **program file** (`projects/_config/CORRECTIONS.md`) only when it applies regardless of which project is active (a workspace-wide behavioral invariant). When unsure → project file (the narrower scope is safer; a genuinely platform-wide pattern surfaces via the cross-project detector in weekly-status-rollup and graduates to a guardrail).

**Session-end writes across the domain boundary.** `SESSION_STATE.md` and `CORRECTIONS.md` live in `projects/_config/` — Layer 2, Cowork-owned. Their writer, write-authority, and class are owned by `pmo-platform/core/disciplines/memory-architecture.md` (the unified memory contract) and are not restated here; the writer it names for both is the operations-rooted session. A `pmo-platform` session writing into `projects/` is prohibited to the agent by [`core/rules/operations-bridge.md`](/core/rules/operations-bridge.md) § Rules for Claude Code (rules 1–2), and the correct response to needing such a write is to relaunch the work in the operations workspace, where it is in-domain ([`core/specs/autonomy-tiers.md`](/core/specs/autonomy-tiers.md) § Irreducible Human Tasks item 7a — Tier 2, not Tier 0). That prohibition is a rule about agent conduct and holds under every posture. **Hook enforcement of it is a separate, posture-dependent axis.** `BLOCK-AUTONOMY-004` gates this direction `warn` → allow + log · `enforce` → block · `off` → allow — but it is declared `MASTER_ENABLE_CLASS="workflow"` and sits *below* the master-activation gate, and master state is `off` by shipped default, so on a fresh install it is inert and contributes nothing at all: not a block, not a warn, not a log row. **The absence of a block is therefore not permission.** Under `enforce` with master enabled the write is blocked, and the session stages the intended update and hands it off per § Quality Standards → Guardrails (Hard Rejections) → **Hook-Blocked → User-Side Handoff**, of which this is a standing instance; under every other posture the session takes the same path by rule rather than waiting to be stopped. Read the `projects/_config/` rows of the Governance File Map and the Layer 2 / Layer 3 tables the same way: they state **ownership**, which is categorical — not the presence of a categorical hook. What varies with posture is enforcement severity, and by default it is absent.

**Rotation focus (per project):** `SESSION_STATE.md` records, per active project, the operator's current **rotation focus** — the focus area the operator is rotating attention through for that project — plus a **weeks-in-focus** count. The focus is stored as an anchor date, `focus_since` (in the file's `YYYY-MM-DD (Day)` form), NOT as a stored counter. Weeks-in-focus is **derived** at read/write time as the whole calendar weeks elapsed since `focus_since` (a week completes on each recurrence of the `focus_since` weekday; a newly-set focus reads 0 in its first partial week). The count therefore advances on the **week boundary, never per session** — multiple sessions in one week do not change it, and a skipped session-end write cannot desync it. At session end, `focus_since` is **overwritten only when the operator changed the rotation focus** that session (resetting the count); otherwise it carries forward unchanged. This honors the `SESSION_STATE.md` single-record-overwrite lifecycle (per `pmo-platform/core/specs/operational-artifact-inventory.md` §8 — bounded to the current handoff, never accreted) and the no-shadow-SSOT invariant (the count is a projection of `focus_since`, not a second stored copy). The focus area is operator-editable at any session.

**Session-start boundary self-check (operations↔engineering).** At session start, before doing the work: if the session is running inside a `pmo-platform/` worktree AND the work is operational / non-code (operator, comms, transcript, tracker, portfolio, or any Cowork-domain task — i.e., the work belongs in the operations workspace), STOP and flag the boundary crossing before proceeding, recommending a relaunch in the operations workspace (`projects/…`). The trigger is the auto-worktree-on-launch mechanism: a non-code session launched against the repo auto-creates a `claude/…` worktree + branch, which (a) crosses the operations↔engineering boundary and (b) opens a latent path for operator content to reach the public repo. **Discriminator — do NOT interfere with legitimate engineering sessions.** A platform-engineering session correctly runs in a repo worktree on a named `fix/` / `feat/` / `chore/` / `release/` branch; the self-check fires ONLY on operational/non-code work, and treats an auto-generated `claude/*` session branch (as distinct from a named engineering branch) as the signal of a mis-launched operational session. When it fires, the correct resolution is relaunch-in-operations-workspace, not "carry on and push carefully."

**Deferred structural teeth (fast-follow).** The reliable structural barrier is rooting the operator entry point outside the repo (the operator-environment convention in the Routing section); the session-start self-check above is the in-session governance backstop. The two runtime *teeth* that would hard-enforce this — a `.claude/settings.json` deny rule for `Write` / `Edit` / `NotebookEdit` and destructive `Bash` (`git commit` / `push`) targeting `pmo-platform/**`, and a PreToolUse push-guard hook that blocks writes resolving inside the repo tree while allowing named engineering branches — are DEFERRED, blocked on the hook-deploy path and on the open subagent-PreToolUse-hook-bypass gap (a guard hook that subagents can bypass is not a barrier). They fast-follow once those land. Until then, the residual accidental-public-push risk is accepted for this release (operator-approved at Collective Review); the governance self-check plus the outside-the-repo entry-point convention are the interim mitigation.

**Staleness rule:** If SESSION_STATE.md `Last Updated` is older than 2 business days, flag: "⚠️ SESSION_STATE.md is stale (last updated: [date]). Recommend full-state refresh before proceeding."

**Cross-account handoff:** When `SWAP_HANDOFF.md` exists and was written more recently than the operator's last session activity, treat its "Last swap" section as authoritative context for resuming work. Surface the swap direction, operator note (if any), and "Active workspace context" / "Suggested resume point" (if present) to the operator at session start. The handoff is metadata + pointers only — read the durable sources it references for full content (git, gh, SESSION_STATE.md, project artifacts). The handoff file is auto-overwritten on every swap; do not modify it manually unless preserving a snapshot.

## Continuous Improvement

GitHub Issues created via the `improvement.yml` template (with any category label per `label-taxonomy.md`) track proposed improvements. Bugs use `bug.yml` (and `bug` label); observations use `observation.yml`. Template selection is typed first — the deterministic type→template rule lives in `release/references/how-to/intake-style-guide.md` §1b.

**Auto-logging rule:** Whenever you identify a gap, inconsistency, broken handoff, missing artifact, or potential improvement during ANY processing — invoke **`intake-desk` Mode C (Ambient Auto-Log)** to author-or-file the work item, rather than writing prose to a static list. Mode C is the non-interactive path: it renders the item against the correct template (see the Tier-selection test below), then acts under the operator's `operator.toml [automation].automation_level` ceiling — `off` surfaces a "held" record (author nothing), `recommend` authors + surfaces the body and the exact create command (create nothing), `bounded_auto` authors → validates → creates without an interactive confirm. A signal whose implied work item is a Tier-0 Irreducible Human Task (governance-file / financial / security-permission / RAID-close, per `core/specs/autonomy-tiers.md`) is never auto-created at any level — Mode C surfaces it for sign-off. Do not wait to be asked, and do not mention a signal in conversation without also routing it through Mode C (at `off`/`recommend` that means surfacing it, not staying silent). This applies to all skills and all session types. The user triages on GitHub — not the skill.

**Runner: NONE — this obligation is a registered named gap, and saying so is the point.** The rule above is a **class 3-O** prose-declared normative predicate per `pmo-platform/core/standards/gate-efficacy-standard.md` § Scope boundary: it names a trigger (a signal identified during any processing) and an act (route it through Mode C), and states no verdict for the negation. **No gate, CI job, or review step executes it**, and none can today — the sanctioned Mode C call and a direct work-item creation are byte-identical tool calls, and no hook reads which skill or mode is executing. Its gate-coverage register row records the gap, and the **declared observable** is the *absence* of the Mode-C agent-authored marker on an agent-created work item, which a reviewer reads at Stage 2 Triage. This label does not weaken the rule; it records that following it is currently a matter of conduct rather than enforcement, so a miss is countable instead of invisible.

**Tier-selection test (when auto-logging):** symmetric content-shape routing — both tiers carry positive triggers; observation-tier is NOT a fallback for unsatisfied proposal-tier (route by what the insight IS, not by what fields can be filled).
- **Observation tier** → use `observation.yml` (with three fields: what is missing, what good looks like in one sentence, which file or section this touches) when EITHER holds: the insight is a placeholder capture not yet stated as a full proposal, OR you cannot fill every required `improvement.yml` field with substantive content.
- **Proposal tier** → use `improvement.yml` when BOTH hold (i.e., neither observation-tier trigger fires): you can state WHAT (problem, constraint, measurable outcome) in ≤3 bullets, AND you can fill every required field of `improvement.yml` with substantive content.
- **Never** author a free-form body that skips template field scaffolding. Every intake ticket must use one of the two templates, field-by-field.

**Release process:** Approved improvements (Issues with `approved` label) are assigned to Milestones and bundled into versioned releases. Implementation plans are generated per release and reviewed before execution. Closed Issues serve as the permanent record in `pmo-platform/release/releases/RELEASE_LOG.md`.
